1 big thing: Lakers flip
 
 By Dan Primack 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Lindsey Bailey/Axios


 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Across the NBA today, owners are smiling.

 Venture capitalist Josh Kushner and former Disney CEO Bob Iger announced they've agreed to buy the Los Angeles Lakers at a $12.5 billion valuation. 🤑 Stunning stat: That's a 25% jump from where the team was valued just 10 months ago in Mark Walter's deal to buy it — still the most ever paid for a pro sports club.

 The big picture: It's a dizzying ownership turnaround for the Lakers, one of pro sports' most iconic franchises.

 Walter — who purchased a controlling stake from Jeanie Buss last October — today called owning the Lakers "one of the great honors of my life" and, in a massive understatement, "an extraordinary investment." 💵 Follow the money: Kushner and Iger would invest individually, with Kushner serving as the controlling owner, per a source.

 The pair's sports-focused investment platform, Thrive Eternal, is expected to invest, but is limited by NBA rules to holding no more than a 20% stake. Expect other investors to join the group. What's next: This is only an agreement, not a finalized deal. It still needs league approval.

